Why Was Hadoop Created?

Imagine you’re working at Netflix.

Every second:

* Millions of users watch movies.
* Millions search for shows.
* Millions pause, rewind, and rate content.

Every action generates data.

Suppose Netflix generates 500 TB of data every day.

Where will you store it?
A normal laptop has about 512 GB of storage.
A powerful server might have 10 TB of storage.

To store 500 TB, you would need around 50 servers, and tomorrow you’ll need 50 more because another 500 TB of data arrives.
So the data keeps growing continuously.

This is called Big Data.

The Traditional Approach
--------------------------
Before Hadoop, companies used one very powerful machine.
This is called Vertical Scaling.

              One Powerful Server

         CPU: 128 Cores
         RAM: 2 TB
         Storage: 500 TB

Everything happens on this one machine.


Problem 1: Cost
----------------

A server like this is extremely expensive.

Instead of buying one massive server, companies realized they could buy many inexpensive commodity servers.

Server 1    ₹1 lakh
Server 2    ₹1 lakh
Server 3    ₹1 lakh
...
100 Servers = ₹1 crore

These servers together provide more storage and computing power, often at a lower cost than a single high-end machine.

Problem 2: Failure
------------------
Imagine all your company data is stored on one server.

         Customer Data

              |
        Powerful Server


 Now imagine the hard disk fails.

          Customer Data

              X

         Server Failed

Everything stops.
Customers cannot access the application.
This is called a Single Point of Failure.   


Problem 3: Performance
---------------------

Suppose you have a 10 TB log file.
One CPU has to read:
Line 1

Line 2

Line 3

...

10 TB

Even if it processes 100 MB/sec, it would take a very long time.
So a single machine becomes a bottleneck.

A Better Idea
----------------

Instead of using one giant computer…
What if we use 100 small computers?

Computer 1

Computer 2

Computer 3

...

Computer 100

Now the work is divided.
Each computer processes only a small part of the data.
If each computer processes 100 GB:

100 Computers

↓

100 GB each

↓

10 TB completed much faster

This is called Distributed Computing.


What Does “Distributed” Mean?

The word distributed simply means:
Split the work among many machines.
For example, imagine 100 students are asked to count the number of words in a library.

Option 1
One student counts every book.
Time: 10 days

Option 2
100 students each count a few books.
Time: 2 hours

The total work is the same.
But because everyone works in parallel, the job finishes much faster.

This is exactly how Hadoop works.


What Hadoop Does
-----------------
Hadoop combines two ideas:

1. Distributed Storage

Instead of storing a file on one computer:

Server 1

Entire File

Hadoop splits the file.

Server 1

Part 1

------------------

Server 2

Part 2

------------------

Server 3

Part 3

2. Distributed Processing

Instead of one CPU processing everything:

CPU

↓

Entire File

Each server processes only its own data.

Server 1

Process Part 1

----------------

Server 2

Process Part 2

----------------

Server 3

Process Part 3

Then Hadoop combines all the results into one final output.


